---
<DocsLayout title="Single-Host Deployment" description="How to test the system on a single host environment" section="Operations" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <h2 id="ids">Dashboard IDs (All Consolidated)</h2>
  <p>All dashboards are consolidated under a single SIEM instance:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>dashboard-ids-alerts</li>
    <li>dashboard-web-threat-overview</li>
    <li>dashboard-threat-decisions</li>
    <li>dashboard-session-analysis</li>
    <li>dashboard-attack-patterns</li>
    <li>dashboard-threat-intel</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="context">SIEM Single-Host Context</h2>
  <p>All dashboards are consolidated under a single SIEM instance. This configuration handles the full lifecycle of the dashboards, relying on build_dashboards.py for creation and export_dashboards.py for distribution/backup.</p>

  <h2 id="notes">Single-Host Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Simplifies deployment by running honeypot, detection, and SIEM components on one host.</li>
    <li>Suitable for evaluation, lab environments, and small-scale deployments.</li>
    <li>May require additional tuning for resource constraints and performance.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In a single-host deployment, all components (reverse proxy, Nginx Lua pipeline, Suricata, Kibana/Elasticsearch, Redis) run on the same host. The routing pipeline diverts suspicious traffic to local honeypot pools, while security events are logged locally and visualized via Kibana on the same host.</p>
</DocsLayout>
